WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.750 align:start position:0%
 
welcome to the newshour russia and  ukraine have signed a deal to reopen  ukrainian ports on the black sea for  grain exports raising hopes of easing  the global food crisis the ceremony in  istanbul was overseen by the u.n  secretary general and the turkish  president who brokered the agreement  grain shipments have been stuck in  ukraine since the start of the russian  invasion in february and that's led to a  rise in global food prices  this is an agreement for the world it  will bring relief for developing  countries on the edge of bankruptcy and  the most vulnerable people on the edge  of famine  and it will help stabilize global food  prices which were already at record  levels even before the war a true  nightmare for developing countries  specifically the initiative we just  signed opens a pass  for significant volumes of commercial  food exports from three key ukrainian  ports in the black sea odessa  chevonnorsk and yuzny  istanbul  this joint step we are taking in  istanbul together with russia and  ukraine will be a new turning point that  will revive the hopes for peace this is  my sincere hope whatever direction the  developments on the field will take the  war will finally end on the negotiating  table this friendly and peaceful  atmosphere that we have established will  be to the benefit of humanity  and this will transform into steps aimed  at ending the war  now in a few moments we'll be speaking  to our diplomatic editor james baze  who's at the united nations in new york  but first let's get reaction out of  ukraine with alan fisher who's in keeve  and of course we will get him shortly we  have slight technical problem there  let's cross over to uh james bayes who's  our diplomatic editor uh in new york and  we'll get to um shortly jane so let's  just begin with the fact that  what's happened it has been very quick  in the last what hour or so  markets normally react very quickly and  i know you're our diplomatic editor but  can you be our economic editor too and  just tell us how they reacted as quickly  as we might have imagined where you are  in new york  i'm afraid so i don't have i have  knowledge of the markets i've just come  out of a diplomatic briefing uh but  certainly the briefing i had was with  the uh the us ambassador who is in  charge of sanctions and part of this  deal is trying to get russian grain and  fertilizer this is the the bit that  favors russia get russian grain and  fertilizer back on global markets  because he's making the point that they  were never subject to sanctions and that  no one should have been stopping the  flow of those now he also told me that  he believes that with regard to shipping  companies and to insurance companies  they should see no problem now in now  that there is this deal and that they  should be able to comply with this deal  and not be wary but of course they are  going to be wary because this deal  involves ships traveling through mined  waters  we're told through channels that have  been clearly identified so i think that  is one of the tests of this how the  shipping market how the insurance market  reacts  and i think there will be a test of  implementation in the coming weeks to  see if this goes ahead it's not going to  happen straight away you're not going to  see a ship that is sailing uh in the  next uh in the next couple of uh  days i think we're talking a couple of  weeks at the earliest for the first ship  of grain and remember that we're talking  about 22 million tons of grain backlog  that has formed in ukraine and caused  much of the global food crisis it's  going to take some time to get all of  that grain out uh experts estimate  probably about four months uh to get all  of that grain out to global markets so  if you thought that it was gonna  alleviate the global food crisis this is  a major breakthrough but nothing's gonna  happen overnight here james you've been  talking behind the scenes to diplomats  for many weeks now about this conflict  uh in ukraine how delicate have the  negotiations been to get to this phase  because we know that from actually  signing an agreement in istanbul  over the next few weeks until they can  get those first ships out as you say  with grain on them anything can happen  oh absolutely it remains a very delicate  situation and it has been throughout i  can tell you that they were talking  about this signing ceremony weeks ago  the first time i heard a potential date  for it uh taking place  and it's been pushed back and pushed  back lots of meetings i think one of the  final meetings took place earlier this  week and that's when president erdogan  of turkey who along with the un has been  the main  broker of this deal met with president  putin they were having a summit with the  iranian president in tehran on the  sidelines of that they had a meeting  that was an important meeting i think  some will be asking the important  question why has russia agreed to this  deal now  what is russia's view on this and does  this suggest in any way that russia is  changing its overall calculations with  regard to the ukrainian war uh certainly  uh the diplomats that i've canvassed on  this believe that russia is getting too  much flack for the global food crisis  and believes that it should be on the  right side of the argument there but  most of them do not believe and even the  secretary general i asked him this a  week ago uh in an open press briefing  they don't believe this is necessarily  now going to translate to peace in  ukraine this is a very specific but very  important deal but unlikely to affect  the ongoing war in the country james  bay's our diplomatic editor thanks james  for the update let's cross over to alan  fishner who we can actually uh get in  touch with now in key valon uh as james  said a very delicate situation that is  only specific the agreement that's been  signed today to grain shipments i mean  the ukrainians themselves have made it  very clear haven't they to this point  that they're not dealing signing a deal  with russia they're signing a deal with  the un  end of  you have the the secretary general and  he's talking in optimistic terms saying  this is growing for optimism  suggesting that somehow this could lead  to more talks that's not something that  the ukrainians are looking at as far as  they're concerned this is one and done  for the time being they see this as a  deal that they have signed with the  united nations and with turkey and  certainly not with russia and we heard  from a senior adviser to the  presidential office on twitter just in  the last couple of hours saying look  it's very clear there will be no russian  vessels in ukrainian territorial waters  under this deal the ukrainians here are  very clear that they see the the  russians as an aggressor nation and we  know that they're approaching the icc  the international criminal court to set  up a whole new investigation with the  intention of charging senior russian  leadership with the  crime of being an aggressor nation they  also say there will be no russian  inspectors in any of the three ukrainian  ports that are under their control if  there to be any searches of ships that  won't happen in ukrainian waters that  will happen in turkish territorial  waters so they are making this  absolutely clear look we have signed  this deal but we will respond to any  provocation from the russians with  military action immediately on late  thursday here in kiev diplomats were  saying look this isn't a done deal yet  even though the united nations and  turkey were saying the wording has been  agreed president zelinski hinted that  there was agreement when he made his  usual address late on thursday night by  saying there was going to be an  agreement in turkey that would help the  country and so they're not seeing this  as a pathway to peace talks not least  because in the last 24 hours or so  sergey lavrov the russian foreign  minister has said that the russian  territorial intention is not just the  donbass region but they see extending it  to kharkiv and also japarisia which is  much more than they had said and and  president zelinski has said that they  intend to inflict a great deal of damage  to the russians in the coming weeks so  there may be optimism that they've  managed to get the two sides to sign an  agreement not with each other but with  third parties but the prospect of this  leading to some sort of agreement in the  near future that will end the war well  that's simply not going to happen ellen  fisher in keith thanks very much

